# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
R = {
'K-1-1': dict(teach=[
'Aim: every child leaves knowing “God is my Father and he loves me.” At five, trust comes before ideas; a child who feels loved by God can later pray, forgive and be brave.',
'Calling God Father tells us he is the source of all things and also pure goodness and loving care for his children (CCC 239). Jesus taught us to say “Our Father” (CCC 2779 asks us to set aside false images).',
'Say: “God takes care of you like a good parent, only even more, and he is always with you.” If asked “Where is God?”: “We can’t see him, but he is with us the way love is with us.”',
'Some children have a father who is absent, harsh or gone. Describe God by what he does (loves, listens, stays) and never ask “who is your dad?” Mention mothers and grandparents who care for them as signs of God’s love.',
'Ask: “Who takes care of you? What does God do for you?” Look for a child who says God loves, helps or is with them. Watch whether they can say “Our Father” in the Sign-of-the-Cross voice.',
]),
'K-1-2': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the world is a gift from a good God, so we say thank you and take care of it. A child who sees creation as a gift will naturally wonder, be grateful and be gentle.',
'The Church professes God as Creator of heaven and earth (CCC 279). He made everything out of love, with order and wisdom (CCC 299), and nothing needed to exist except that he wanted it.',
'Say: “Before anything was, God was. He said it and it was, because he is God.” If a child asks “Who made God?”: “Nobody. God has always been, he had no beginning. That is what makes him God.”',
'Do not set Genesis against science; a five-year-old may hear dinosaurs, space or evolution at home. Say: “God made it all; people study how.” Avoid mocking any family’s view.',
'Walk the room naming a made thing and ask: “Who made this?” Listen for “God” and for a thank-you. Invite each child to name one creation they are thankful for.',
]),
'K-1-3': dict(teach=[
'Aim: each child knows “God made me on purpose and loves me just as I am.” That sense of worth protects them from comparing, bullying and shame, and it grounds all later moral teaching.',
'Humans are made in God’s image, a person who is someone, not something, able to know and love God (CCC 355–357). The soul and body together make one person, loved by God from the first moment.',
'Say: “You are not an accident. God thought of you and wanted you, and nobody else is just like you.” If asked “Why did God make me?”: “To know him, love him and be happy with him.”',
'Be careful with children who have disabilities, health issues, adoption or a hard family story. Never compare bodies, abilities or looks. Say “every child”, and mean it. Avoid “God made me perfect” as a comparison.',
'Ask: “Who made you? Does God love you?” Look for children who answer with confidence. Notice anyone who hangs back or says something negative about themselves, and affirm them privately.',
]),
'K-2-1': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child hears that God loved us so much he sent his own Son, who became a real baby. This is the heart of Christmas: Christmas is about Jesus, not only about gifts.',
'The Son of God became man for our salvation (CCC 456). Born in a stable to a poor family, he was first shown to humble shepherds (CCC 525). He is truly God and truly man.',
'Say: “Jesus is God, and he came as a baby so we could be close to him.” If asked “How can a baby be God?”: “God can do anything; he chose to be small because he loves us.”',
'Avoid blending the story with Santa or cartoon scenery; keep it as true history. Be aware of children whose families do not celebrate Christmas or who struggle, and keep the focus on welcome.',
'Ask: “Who was born? Who was his mother?” Let a child place figures in a manger. Listen for “Jesus”, “Mary” and “baby”, and see if they say Jesus is God’s Son.',
]),
'K-2-2': dict(teach=[
'Aim: each child hears “Jesus wants me, and I am welcome to him.” Young children often feel they must be quiet or good first; the Gospel shows Jesus welcoming them as they are.',
'Jesus welcomed children and said the kingdom belongs to those who receive it like a child (Mark 10:13–16). Childlike trust is the model for prayer (CCC 2785, 526).',
'Say: “Jesus is happy when you come to him. You don’t have to be perfect.” If asked “Is Jesus here now?”: “Yes, he is with us always, and we meet him in prayer and the Mass.”',
'Some children feel unwanted, move between homes or have been told they are bad. Use warm eye contact, learn each name and avoid singling anyone out as the “good” child. Hold the story gently.',
'Ask each child, “Does Jesus love you?” and watch for smiles or a yes. Ask what Jesus did with the children; they should say he hugged or blessed them, not sent them away.',
]),
'K-2-3': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child learns Jesus cares about people who are hurting and that we can bring our needs to him. Compassion and trust in prayer begin with this simple picture.',
'Jesus’ healings are signs that the kingdom has come and that he is sent by the Father (CCC 547–548). They call us to faith; they do not promise that every illness will go away now.',
'Say: “Jesus cares when we are sick or sad, and we can tell him. He is always with us.” If asked “Why doesn’t Jesus heal Grandma?”: “I don’t know all God’s ways, but he loves her, and we can keep praying.”',
'Do not promise a cure or imply sickness means a lack of faith. Children may have a seriously ill parent or sibling; keep the focus on Jesus’ presence and prayer. Tell the parents if a child opens up about a deep fear.',
'Ask: “Who can we ask when we are sick or sad?” Invite a short prayer for someone they know. Look for children who name a person to pray for and who say Jesus helps.',
]),
'K-3-1': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child meets Mary as the mother who said yes to God, and who is also a mother to us. A trusting yes to God is a model for a child’s everyday obedience and love.',
'Mary is the mother of Jesus and so Mother of God; the Church honors her as our mother in the order of grace (CCC 495, 501, 963). We honor her and do not worship her; worship is for God alone.',
'Say: “Mary loves you like a mother and she brings you to Jesus.” If asked “Is Mary God?”: “No, Mary is Jesus’ mother and our mother. She is a person like us, and God gave her a special job.”',
'Some Christian families do not pray to Mary, and some children have lost a mother. Speak simply and invitingly, not defensively, and let Mary be a gift, never a requirement.',
'Ask: “What did Mary say to the angel?” Look for “yes”. Pray the first line of the Hail Mary together and see who joins in unprompted.',
]),
'K-3-2': dict(teach=[
'Aim: holiness is for ordinary people, even children. Saints are friends who show us how to love God and who help us now, so being good feels possible, not far away.',
'The Church canonizes people who lived heroic virtue (CCC 828), and those in heaven keep interceding for us (CCC 956). Asking a saint to pray for us is like asking a friend, not replacing Christ.',
'Say: “Saints loved Jesus very much, and now they are with God and pray for us.” If asked “Where are they?”: “With God in heaven, where there is joy and no sadness.”',
'Avoid presenting saints as perfect from birth or only as martyrs; skip gory details. Some children may be frightened by talk of death. Pick a gentle saint such as Francis, Thérèse or a child saint.',
'Ask: “Who is a saint you know? What did that saint do for God?” Listen for any specific act of love. Notice whether children can say that saints once were children too.',
]),
'K-3-3': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child feels safe and not alone because God sends a helper. This is a source of comfort at bedtime and in worries, as long as it stays rooted in God’s care.',
'Angels are spiritual beings without bodies, God’s servants and messengers (CCC 328). Each believer has an angel as protector and guide from infancy to death (CCC 336).',
'Say: “Your angel is God’s helper who watches over you and prays to God for you.” If asked “Can I see my angel?”: “Usually not, because angels are spirits, but they are real.”',
'Do not use the angel as a threat (“your angel is watching you do wrong”); that turns comfort into fear. Skip cherub imagery; angels are not dead people and are not babies with wings.',
'Ask: “Who sends the angels to help us? What can we say to our angel?” See if children can say a short “Angel of God” line or name God as the one who sent the angel.',
]),
'K-4-1': dict(teach=[
'Aim: each child knows “I belong to God’s family, the Church.” Belonging comes before understanding, and it supports faith better than rules in the early years.',
'The Church is the people called together by God (CCC 751), and Baptism makes us members of Christ’s Body and the one People of God (CCC 1267). The Church is people, not only a building.',
'Say: “The Church is God’s family. We belong because we are baptized.” If asked “Am I in the family if I’m not baptized?”: “God loves you always, and we welcome you, and Baptism is how a person joins God’s family.”',
'Children may be unbaptized, come from mixed-faith homes or family members who no longer attend. Never say who is “in” or “out.” Welcome every child warmly, and speak of others with respect.',
'Ask: “Who is in God’s family? Name someone in our parish.” Look for a child who names a priest, a friend, or says “all of us.” Notice whether they say “we” rather than “they.”',
]),
'K-4-2': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child learns that church is a special place because Jesus is present there, so we act with quiet respect and joy. Reverence is learned by doing, at this age.',
'The church building is a place for the assembled people to worship (CCC 1179), and the tabernacle holds the Blessed Sacrament for adoration, where Jesus is really present (CCC 1183).',
'Say: “This is God’s house, and Jesus is truly here in the tabernacle. We use quiet voices, because we are with him.” If asked “Is he really in there?”: “Yes. We believe it because Jesus promised.”',
'Avoid making quietness a punishment or demanding stillness beyond a five-year-old’s ability. Some children are used to noisy worship or none at all. Teach the walk and the bow as a way to greet a friend.',
'Take a short walk through the church and ask each child to point to the altar, cross and tabernacle. See who whispers and who genuflects or bows without being told.',
]),
'K-4-3': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child learns Sunday is special because Jesus rose on that day, and that Mass is where God’s family meets him. Joy matters more than obligation at this age.',
'Sunday is the Lord’s Day, the day of the Resurrection and the new creation (CCC 2174). The Sunday Eucharist is at the heart of the Church’s life (CCC 2177).',
'Say: “On Sunday we go to Mass to thank Jesus, who rose from the dead.” If asked “Why must we go?”: “Because Jesus invites his family to come, and it’s the best way to thank him.”',
'Do not shame children for missing Mass; they rarely choose it. Some have parents who work Sundays or do not attend. Celebrate the Sunday they do come, and invite families without pressure.',
'Ask: “What day is Mass? What do we do there?” Look for answers like sing, pray, listen or thank. Notice whether any child mentions Jesus rising.',
]),
'K-5-1': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child understands that in Baptism God made them his own child and part of his family. Identity as a beloved child of God is the root of Christian life.',
'Baptism is the gateway to life in the Spirit and frees us from sin, making us children of God and members of Christ (CCC 1213). The white garment and candle show we belong to Christ the light (CCC 1243, 1262).',
'Say: “When you were baptized, water was poured and God made you his child.” If asked “What if I wasn’t baptized?”: “God loves you very much. Ask your parents about Baptism; the Church welcomes you.”',
'Some children are not baptized, or Baptism was in another church. Do not imply they are less loved. Never use “saved” or “unsaved” language. Invite parents privately to speak with the priest.',
'Show a candle, water and white cloth and ask what each means. Look for “God’s child”, “light” and “family.” Ask who has a baptism day to share.',
]),
'K-5-2': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child learns that at Mass Jesus gives himself to us and that we love and respect him in this gift. Reverence grows from knowing who Jesus is, not from fear.',
'At the Last Supper Jesus gave us the Eucharist (CCC 1323), and after the consecration his Body and Blood, soul and divinity are truly, really and substantially present under the appearances of bread and wine (CCC 1374).',
'Say: “At Mass the bread and wine become Jesus, though they still look the same. He gives himself to us.” If asked “Is it a symbol?”: “It is a sign, and also really Jesus. That’s what Jesus said.”',
'Do not make children feel excluded if they do not yet receive; invite a spiritual communion. Avoid tasting games or ordinary-food comparisons, and never joke about the host.',
'Ask: “Who is in the tabernacle? What does the priest say?” Watch how they hold their hands and bow. See if any say “Jesus” without being prompted.',
]),
'K-5-3': dict(teach=[
'Aim: a child knows that mistakes are not the end, and that God forgives when we are sorry. This prevents a hidden fear that God’s love must be earned.',
'In Penance we receive God’s mercy and are reconciled with him and the Church (CCC 1422). The parable of the merciful father shows the heart of repentance (CCC 1439).',
'Say: “When we do wrong, we can say sorry and God always forgives, like the father who ran to his son.” If asked “What if I do it again?”: “God still forgives. We try again, and he helps us.”',
'Avoid fear, guilt or lists of sins. Do not suggest God is angry or keeping score. Some children are scrupulous or punished severely at home; keep your voice calm and the focus on God’s welcome.',
'Role-play a small hurt: one child says sorry and another says “I forgive you.” Ask “How did God feel about the son?” and listen for “happy” or “hugged him.”',
]),
'K-6-1': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child sees that following Jesus means loving God and loving others in small daily actions. Love is a habit practiced at snack time and on the playground.',
'Jesus summed up the Law in two commands: love God with all your heart, and love your neighbor as yourself (Mark 12:29–31; CCC 2055, 2196). Love of neighbor flows from love of God.',
'Say: “Love God first, and show it by being kind to others.” If asked “Do I have to love everyone?”: “We try to be kind to everyone. Loving means wanting good for them, even when it’s hard.”',
'Do not promise that kindness always feels easy, or teach children to ignore being hurt. A child can be kind and still tell a grown-up if someone is unkind. Avoid ranking kids by how nice they are.',
'Watch the day for a real moment of sharing or helping and name it: “You loved your neighbor.” Ask children to say one way to love God and one way to love a friend.',
]),
'K-6-2': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child learns truth is safe and good, and that honesty keeps friendships trusting. Five-year-olds mix pretend and lies, so gently teach the difference.',
'The eighth commandment forbids misrepresenting the truth, because God is truth (CCC 2464), and truthfulness is the virtue of being true in word and deed (CCC 2468). Jesus says the truth sets us free.',
'Say: “God wants us to say what is true, even when it’s hard, and he helps us be brave.” If asked “Is it a lie to pretend?”: “No, play pretend is fun. A lie is trying to trick someone.”',
'Do not punish honesty harshly; a child who is scolded for confessing learns to hide. Fear of consequences drives lying. Be honest yourself, and thank children for owning up.',
'Present a small scenario (a spilled juice) and ask what to say. Look for “I did it” and “I’m sorry.” Notice who says a lie is wrong because it hurts.',
]),
'K-6-3': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child has words to say sorry and a first understanding of forgiving. Reconciliation is learned in tiny daily hurts, and it grows from knowing Jesus forgives us first.',
'We ask forgiveness and forgive because God forgave us; in the Our Father we ask to be forgiven as we forgive (CCC 2843, 2842). Forgiving is a choice to be kind, not necessarily a feeling.',
'Say: “When we hurt someone, we say sorry. When someone hurts us, we forgive, because Jesus forgave us.” If asked “What if they hurt me again?”: “Tell a grown-up. Forgiving doesn’t mean letting it keep happening.”',
'Never force a child to hug or say “it’s okay” to someone who hurt them. Forgiveness is not the same as staying in unsafe situations. If a child describes being harmed, tell a trusted adult right away.',
'Observe a real conflict and coach: “What can you say?” Look for a clear “I’m sorry” and a returning “I forgive you,” and a child who goes back to play.',
]),
'K-7-1': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child learns that prayer is simply talking and listening to God, anywhere, in any words. The goal is relationship, not performance or the right formula.',
'Prayer is raising the mind and heart to God (CCC 2559), and in the New Covenant it is the living relationship of God’s children with their Father (CCC 2565). Jesus invites us to pray in simple trust.',
'Say: “Prayer is talking to God like a friend, and also being quiet with him.” If asked “Does God answer?”: “Yes, he always listens. Sometimes he answers in ways we don’t expect, and he always loves us.”',
'Avoid making prayer a long, still obligation. Some children have never prayed; others pray scripted prayers only. Accept short, wiggly, silly-sweet prayers. Do not promise God will give every request.',
'Invite a thirty-second quiet and then a spoken prayer: “Thank you, God for…” See who can talk to God in their own words and who needs a sentence to begin.',
]),
'K-7-2': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child learns the Our Father as Jesus’ own prayer and begins to say it with understanding. Memorized prayer gives words for a lifetime and ties the child to the whole Church.',
'The Lord’s Prayer is given by Jesus himself to his disciples and to the Church (CCC 2759, 2765). It makes us children who can say “Our Father” and ask in trust.',
'Say: “Jesus taught us this prayer to talk to our Father.” Explain “hallowed” as “holy” and “daily bread” as what we need. If asked “Why ‘our’?”: “Because God is Father to all of us together.”',
'Do not push perfect memorization; mumbling the words is common and fine. Some families say it with different wording (for example, trespasses or debts). Accept the form used at home as well as at church.',
'Pray it slowly with gestures and note who joins in. Ask “Who taught us this prayer?” and look for “Jesus.” Ask which line they like best.',
]),
'K-7-3': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child gains two prayer habits they can do alone: the Sign of the Cross and a thank-you before eating. Small repeated habits become a prayer life.',
'The Sign of the Cross begins and ends prayer, naming the Trinity and calling on Christ’s grace (CCC 2157). Grace before and after meals is a traditional rhythm of prayer (CCC 2698).',
'Say: “The Sign of the Cross tells God we belong to him; Father, Son and Holy Spirit.” If asked “Why three?”: “It’s one God in three persons. It is a mystery we say and love; we don’t need to understand it all.”',
'Do not scold left–right mix-ups, and mind that Eastern Christians cross themselves right to left. Some homes do not say grace; invite, don’t shame. Avoid hurried “bless-us” rushing.',
'Practice slowly and watch hands. Ask “Why do we say thank you at meals?” and look for “God gave us food.” See who tries it at snack without a reminder.',
]),
'K-8-1': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child learns that waiting can be joyful and helps us prepare to welcome Jesus. This counters the idea that Christmas begins in November with shopping.',
'In Advent the Church recalls the long expectation of the Messiah and renews desire for Christ’s coming (CCC 524). It is a season in the Church year (CCC 1163).',
'Say: “Advent means getting ready for Jesus. Each candle on the wreath tells us Jesus is nearer.” If asked “Why wait?”: “Good gifts are worth getting ready for, and we get our hearts ready.”',
'Families may have trees up early or not celebrate. Do not criticize. Avoid making Advent a countdown to gifts. Keep it about kindness, prayer and light, within a five-year-old’s sense of time.',
'Ask: “How many candles will we light this week? What are we getting ready for?” Look for “Jesus” and a small kind act they plan. See who can name the wreath’s purple and pink.',
]),
'K-8-2': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child sees Lent as a time of loving prayer and sharing and Easter as the greatest joy, because Jesus is alive. Easter, not Lent, is the goal.',
'Lent is a penitential season of prayer, almsgiving and self-denial (CCC 1438). Easter is the feast of feasts, the Resurrection of Christ (CCC 1169, 540).',
'Say: “In Lent we pray, share and give up something small to love Jesus more. At Easter, Jesus rose, and we are glad.” If asked “Did Jesus die?”: “Yes, and then he rose to new life. He is alive today.”',
'Do not dwell on the Crucifixion’s violence. Skip fasting for young children; choose small sacrifices like sharing a toy. Children who have lost a loved one may be sensitive about death; keep Easter hope central.',
'Ask: “What is one small thing we can do for Lent? What happened at Easter?” Listen for “share”, “pray” and “Jesus is alive.” Notice who greets with Alleluia.',
]),
'K-8-3': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child learns that ordinary days matter to God and that faith grows slowly. Green for growth supports the idea of daily steady love, not only big feasts.',
'Ordinary Time is the long stretch of the year when the Church follows Jesus’ life and teaching week by week (CCC 1163, 1168). The year of the Lord is filled with Easter’s light.',
'Say: “Green is for growing. Every day we grow a little in loving God.” If asked “Why isn’t it special?”: “Every day is a gift from God. Ordinary days are when we practice loving him.”',
'Avoid implying Ordinary Time is boring or lesser. Gardening comparisons may not fit children without seeds or yards; use windowsill plants. Don’t blame a child whose plant did not grow.',
'Plant or look at a seed and ask: “How do we grow? Who helps?” Look for answers such as sun, water and God. Notice who offers a thank-you for something small.',
]),
'K-L1': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child has words to say sorry and knows Jesus always forgives; forgiving others is learned from being forgiven. This builds the base for Confession and for peacemaking at school.',
'In the sacrament of Penance God’s mercy pardons the sinner and reconciles him with the Church (CCC 1422–1424). In the Our Father we ask to be forgiven as we forgive (CCC 2842–2845).',
'Say: “I’m sorry. Please forgive me,” and “I forgive you.” If asked “Does God forgive when I’m not sorry?”: “God always loves us. When we say sorry, we are ready to receive his forgiveness.”',
'Do not force a premature “I’m sorry.” Insincere apologies teach little; model and invite. Avoid shaming, and watch for children who see themselves as bad. Safety first: report harm to a grown-up.',
'Role-play a toy-taking scene; ask “What can we say?” Look for a clear apology and an offer to help. See who says “Jesus forgives me” unprompted.',
]),
'K-L2': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child connects gratitude and sharing with God’s generosity, so giving is joy, not loss. Thankfulness shapes how a child sees the world and the Mass.',
'Eucharist means thanksgiving (CCC 1328), and every event can become an offering of thanks (CCC 2638). We ask for daily bread, trusting the Father (CCC 2828), and we help the poor (CCC 2443).',
'Say: “Everything good comes from God, so we say thank you, and we share what we have.” If asked “Why share my toy?”: “Sharing makes both people happy, and it’s how God shares with us.”',
'Do not force sharing of a special possession or shame the child who hesitates; let them choose. Some families have little. Avoid making thanks a comparison with children who have less.',
'Ask each child to name one thing they are thankful for and one way to share. Look for a thank-you addressed to God, not just a list. Watch for spontaneous sharing at snack.',
]),
'K-L3': dict(teach=[
'Aim: the child learns that fear is normal, God is near, and there are things to do: breathe, pray, tell a grown-up. This gives real help for nights, doctors and new places.',
'God cares for each child, whose smallest needs the Father knows (CCC 305). Each person has an angel guardian (CCC 336), and prayer is trust in our Father (CCC 2559).',
'Say: “God is with you, and your angel too. Say: Jesus, I trust in you.” If asked “Why do scary things happen?”: “Sometimes bad things happen. God is still with us and helps us through them.”',
'Don’t say “there’s nothing to be afraid of” or shame a child for crying. Some are carrying real fears, like a hospital stay or conflict at home. Pass concerns to parents, and report signs of harm.',
'Ask: “What can you do when you are scared?” Look for the three steps: breathe, pray, tell a grown-up. Invite a short “Jesus, I trust in you” and see who can say it calmly.',
]),
}
